const e={id:16,sections:[{id:"intro",type:"content",title:"David and Goliath",content:[{type:"text",value:"The hero-maker brain doesn't just cast us as heroes — it casts us as **underdogs**. We construct narratives of moral struggle against formidable odds. This is the David and Goliath pattern, and it's hardwired into how we understand ourselves."},{type:"callout",variant:"key-concept",value:"We naturally frame our struggles as David vs. Goliath — the small, righteous individual against the powerful, corrupt system. This framing provides meaning and motivation, but it can also distort reality."},{type:"text",value:"This chapter explores how the underdog narrative shapes identity, and what happens during the rare moments when belief systems actually collapse."}]},{id:"rarity-of-change",type:"content",title:"The Rarity of Belief Change",content:[{type:"text",value:"While humans can think rationally, **significant identity-level belief shifts** are rare exceptions. The model-defending brain ensures that most challenges to our worldview are dismissed, reinterpreted, or ignored."},{type:"text",value:"When identity-level change does happen, it's:"},{type:"list",items:["**Painful** — feels like part of the self is dying","**Public** — often triggered by undeniable external evidence","**Transformative** — changes not just one belief but the entire framework","**Rare** — most people live their whole lives defending their models"]},{type:"callout",variant:"insight",value:"This is why character transformation in fiction is so powerful. We're witnessing something that almost never happens in real life — someone actually changing who they are."}]},{id:"case-study-lynas",type:"content",title:"Case Study: Mark Lynas",content:[{type:"text",value:"Mark Lynas was an eco-terrorist with the Earth First movement. He sabotaged genetically modified crops, believing he was fighting to save the planet from corporate destruction. Classic David vs. Goliath."},{type:"text",value:"Then he actually read the science. He discovered that GM foods were safe, and that his movement was causing harm — preventing technology that could help feed people. His entire hero narrative collapsed."},{type:"quote",value:`Lynas underwent a painful public transformation. He had to admit that he — the righteous David — had actually been part of the problem. The "Goliath" he'd been fighting was, in some ways, doing more good than he was.`,attribution:"Course analysis"},{type:"text",value:"What made change possible for Lynas? He couldn't ignore the evidence forever. And crucially, he found a way to construct a **new** hero narrative: now he was the brave truth-teller standing against his former allies."}]},{id:"quiz-1",type:"quiz",quizType:"formative",title:"Belief Change",questions:[{id:"ch16-q1",type:"multiple-choice",question:"Identity-level belief change is rare because:",options:[{id:"a",text:"People are too lazy to reconsider their views",correct:!1},{id:"b",text:"The model-defending brain interprets challenges as threats to the self",correct:!0},{id:"c",text:"Most beliefs are actually correct",correct:!1},{id:"d",text:"Society punishes people who change their minds",correct:!1}],feedback:{correct:"Exactly! The model-defending brain treats belief challenges as attacks on identity itself. Change means a kind of death.",incorrect:"Identity-level change is rare because the **model-defending brain** protects beliefs as if they were the self."},conceptKey:"belief-change-rarity"}]},{id:"hero-maker-narrative",type:"content",title:"The Hero-Maker Narrative Structure",content:[{type:"text",value:"The hero-maker brain constructs narratives of **moral struggle against formidable odds**. These stories provide meaning and urgency:"},{type:"list",items:["We are the small, righteous force (David)","We face a large, powerful, corrupt opponent (Goliath)","Our struggle is morally necessary and heroic","Victory would be a triumph of good over evil"]},{type:"text",value:"This framing is psychologically satisfying — it makes us feel important and righteous. But it can distort reality. Sometimes we're not David. Sometimes we're not even fighting Goliath. Sometimes we ARE Goliath."},{type:"callout",variant:"insight",value:"Both sides of most conflicts see themselves as David. Both believe they're the underdog fighting injustice. This is why conflicts are so intractable — everyone thinks they're the hero."}]},{id:"citizen-kane",type:"content",title:"Case Study: Citizen Kane",content:[{type:"text",value:"*Citizen Kane* illustrates the internal contradictions of the hero-maker narrative:"},{type:"text",value:"Kane begins as a crusader for the common people — the classic David fighting the Goliath of corrupt wealth and power. He runs a newspaper that champions the disadvantaged."},{type:"text",value:"But Kane is also desperate for approval, control, and love. His hero narrative (fighting for the people) conflicts with his actual behavior (manipulating, controlling, abandoning principles for personal gain)."},{type:"quote",value:"Kane's tragedy is the gap between his hero-maker narrative and his actual self. He tells himself he's fighting for the little guy while becoming the very thing he claims to oppose.",attribution:"Course analysis"},{type:"text",value:"This gap — between the story we tell about ourselves and who we actually are — is the engine of tragic character drama."}]},{id:"quiz-2",type:"quiz",quizType:"formative",title:"David and Goliath",questions:[{id:"ch16-q2",type:"fill-blank",question:"Complete this insight about conflict:",sentence:"Both sides of most conflicts see themselves as _____. Both believe they're fighting _____.",blanks:[{id:"b1",answer:"David",options:["David","heroes","victims","underdogs"]},{id:"b2",answer:"injustice",options:["injustice","evil","tyranny","oppression"]}],feedback:{correct:"Yes! This is why conflicts are so intractable — mutual David narratives.",incorrect:`The insight is: "Both sides see themselves as David. Both believe they're fighting injustice."`},conceptKey:"mutual-david-narratives"},{id:"ch16-q3",type:"multiple-choice",question:"Mark Lynas was able to change his beliefs because:",options:[{id:"a",text:"Someone he trusted told him he was wrong",correct:!1},{id:"b",text:"He couldn't ignore the evidence and found a new hero narrative",correct:!0},{id:"c",text:"He stopped caring about the environment",correct:!1},{id:"d",text:"He was forced to change by legal consequences",correct:!1}],feedback:{correct:"Exactly! Change required both undeniable evidence AND a new hero narrative (brave truth-teller).",incorrect:"Lynas changed because the evidence became undeniable, and he found a **new hero narrative** to replace the old one."},conceptKey:"belief-change-mechanism"}]},{id:"exercise",type:"exercise",title:"The Belief Shift Arc",exercises:[{id:"ch16-ex1",type:"structured-template",title:"Design a Belief Collapse",instructions:"Design a character arc where the protagonist's core belief is shattered mid-story.",fields:[{id:"original-narrative",label:"Original David vs. Goliath Narrative",placeholder:'What story does the character tell themselves? Who is their "Goliath"?'},{id:"evidence",label:"The Undeniable Discovery",placeholder:"What evidence forces them to question everything?"},{id:"collapse",label:"The Collapse",placeholder:"How does it feel when their hero narrative falls apart?"},{id:"new-narrative",label:"New Narrative (or Tragic Refusal)",placeholder:"Do they construct a new hero narrative, or refuse to change? What happens?"}],xpReward:200}]},{id:"quiz-summative",type:"quiz",quizType:"summative",title:"Chapter 16 Assessment",passingScore:70,questions:[{id:"ch16-s1",type:"multiple-choice",conceptKey:"david-goliath-narrative",question:"The David vs. Goliath narrative pattern:",options:[{id:"a",text:"Is unique to Western storytelling traditions",correct:!1},{id:"b",text:"Is a natural product of the hero-maker brain seeking meaning",correct:!0},{id:"c",text:"Only applies to physical conflicts",correct:!1},{id:"d",text:"Is always an accurate representation of reality",correct:!1}],feedback:{correct:"Yes! The David narrative emerges naturally from how the hero-maker brain constructs meaning and purpose.",incorrect:"The David vs. Goliath pattern is a **natural product of the hero-maker brain** seeking meaning in struggle."}},{id:"ch16-s2",type:"ordering",conceptKey:"belief-change",question:"Order the stages of Mark Lynas's transformation:",items:[{id:"a",text:"Confronted scientific evidence he couldn't dismiss"},{id:"b",text:'Sabotaged GM crops as eco-terrorist "David"'},{id:"c",text:"Public transformation and new hero narrative"},{id:"d",text:"Painful recognition that he was wrong"}],correctOrder:["b","a","d","c"],feedback:{correct:"Perfect! Activist → Evidence → Pain → New narrative.",incorrect:"Order: Eco-terrorism → Undeniable evidence → Painful recognition → New hero narrative."}},{id:"ch16-s3",type:"multiple-choice",conceptKey:"citizen-kane",question:"Citizen Kane's tragedy illustrates:",options:[{id:"a",text:"The dangers of wealth and power",correct:!1},{id:"b",text:"The gap between hero-maker narrative and actual self",correct:!0},{id:"c",text:"How journalism corrupts good people",correct:!1},{id:"d",text:"The importance of childhood trauma",correct:!1}],feedback:{correct:"Exactly! Kane's tragedy is the gap between the story he tells about himself and who he actually is.",incorrect:"Kane's tragedy is the **gap** between his hero-maker narrative (champion of the people) and his actual behavior."}},{id:"ch16-s4",type:"matching",conceptKey:"belief-change-elements",question:"Match each element to its role in belief change:",pairs:[{id:"a",left:"Undeniable evidence",right:"Makes dismissal impossible"},{id:"b",left:"New hero narrative",right:"Provides alternative identity"},{id:"c",left:"Public transformation",right:"Commits to new position"},{id:"d",left:"Pain of recognition",right:"Necessary cost of change"}],feedback:{correct:"Perfect! Each element plays a crucial role in making belief change possible.",incorrect:"Evidence makes dismissal impossible; new narrative provides identity; public transformation commits; pain is the cost."}}]},{id:"summary",type:"content",title:"Chapter Summary",content:[{type:"text",value:"## Key Takeaways from Chapter 16"},{type:"list",items:["**David vs. Goliath** — the hero-maker brain naturally constructs underdog narratives","**Belief change is rare** — model defending keeps most people consistent for life","**Both sides see themselves as David** — mutual hero narratives make conflicts intractable","**Change requires** — undeniable evidence + new hero narrative","**Tragic gap** — between the story we tell and who we actually are"]},{type:"callout",variant:"next",value:"**Next up**: Chapter 17 — How Flawed Characters Create Meaning. The capstone of Part 2 — synthesizing everything into a framework for meaningful character-driven storytelling."}]}]};export{e as chapter16};
